CH = dict(num=2, title="Exploring Mixtures and their Separation", tag="Chemistry", theme="Solutions, concentration, separation techniques, suspensions, colloids, Tyndall effect",
intro="Sugar from sugarcane, malaria tests from a drop of blood, clean water from muddy water — all depend on **separating mixtures**. This chapter classifies mixtures as **solutions, suspensions and colloids**, shows how to express the **concentration** of a solution in percentages, and explains which technique (crystallization, distillation, chromatography, separating funnel, sublimation, centrifugation, coagulation) suits which mixture and why.",
big_questions=["Why do suspended particles settle in muddy water but not in milk?", "How is evaporation different from boiling?", "Why do we see bright rays of sunlight through gaps in the leaves of a dense tree?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "2.1 Classifying Mixtures"),
("ul", ["**Homogeneous mixture (solution):** uniform composition throughout — a well-stirred sugar solution is equally sweet in the first and last sip. Examples: vinegar (acetic acid in water), soda water (carbon dioxide in water), air, brass. A solution always remains homogeneous.",
 "**Heterogeneous mixture:** composition is not uniform; the parts can be seen or settle. Examples: sand in water, oil and water, smoke, fog, iron filings and sulfur.",
 "Oil and water are **heterogeneous** (they form separate layers)."]),
("h3", "Activity 2.1 — Salt, chalk powder and milk in water"),
("steps", [("Step 1 — Prepare", "A: a spatula of common salt in 50 mL water. B: a spatula of chalk powder in 50 mL water. C: a few drops of milk in 50 mL water. Stir each well."),
 ("Step 2 — Observe", "Check whether particles are visible; pass a laser beam through each beaker and look from the side (never look into the beam — it damages the eyes); leave undisturbed; then filter each and look for residue.")]),
("table", ["Observation", "A: Salt + water", "B: Chalk + water", "C: Milk + water"], [["Particles visible?", "No — clear", "Yes — cloudy, visible", "Not visible, but looks milky"], ["Laser path visible?", "No", "Yes", "Yes"], ["On standing", "Nothing settles", "Chalk settles at the bottom", "Nothing settles"], ["Residue on filter paper", "None", "Yes", "None"]]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "So the three mixtures are **different types**: A is a **solution**, B is a **suspension**, C is a **colloid**. The differences lie mainly in **particle size**."),

("h2", "2.2 Solutions and Their Concentration"),
("ul", ["A solution is formed when a **solute** (substance that dissolves) is mixed with a **solvent** (substance that dissolves the solute). In sugar + water, sugar is the solute and water the solvent.",
 "**Concentration** = the amount of solute dissolved in a given amount of solvent or solution.",
 "Right proportion matters: **ORS** (oral rehydration solution) works only when fixed amounts of salt and sugar are used in a fixed amount of water — not every sugary drink is ORS. Too little pesticide fails to protect a crop, too much damages crop, soil and environment.",
 "ORS was developed and popularised by the Indian paediatrician **Dilip Mahalanabis**; it has saved millions of lives from dehydration caused by diarrhoea and cholera."]),
("h3", "Three ways to express concentration (percentage)"),
("table", ["Method", "Formula", "Meaning", "Used for"], [
 ["Mass by mass % (% m/m or % w/w)", "(Mass of solute ÷ Mass of solution) × 100", "Grams of solute in 100 g of solution", "Homogeneous and some heterogeneous mixtures; milk powder, spice mixes, nutrition labels"],
 ["Mass by volume % (% m/v)", "(Mass of solute ÷ Volume of solution in mL) × 100", "Grams of solute in 100 mL of solution", "Medicines, laboratory liquids (5% glucose; 0.9% saline)"],
 ["Volume by volume % (% v/v)", "(Volume of solute ÷ Volume of solution) × 100", "mL of solute in 100 mL of solution", "Two miscible liquids — perfumes, cosmetics, vinegar"]]),
("note", "FORMULA", "Mass of solution = mass of solute + mass of solvent (not the solvent alone). In industry, weight and mass are used interchangeably, so % m/m and % w/w are numerically equal."),
("h3", "Worked examples"),
("table", ["Example", "Working", "Answer"], [
 ["10 g salt in 90 g water — % m/m?", "Mass of solution = 10 + 90 = 100 g; (10 ÷ 100) × 100", "10 % m/m"],
 ["5 g glucose made up to 100 mL — % m/v?", "(5 ÷ 100) × 100", "5 % m/v"],
 ["1 mL pesticide in 100 mL spray — % v/v?", "(1 ÷ 100) × 100", "1 % v/v"],
 ["Talcum powder with 4 % m/m zinc oxide: zinc oxide in 300 g?", "4 ÷ 100 × 300", "12 g"],
 ["2 tablespoons (15 mL each) concentrate in 150 mL juice — % v/v?", "Concentrate = 30 mL; (30 ÷ 150) × 100", "20 % v/v"],
 ["Make 5 % v/v vinegar from glacial (100 %) acetic acid", "Take 5 mL glacial acetic acid and add water to make 100 mL total (the same ratio for any quantity)", "5 mL in 100 mL"],
 ["Cake mix: 75 g sugar, 420 g flour, 5 g sodium hydrogencarbonate", "Total = 500 g → sugar 15 %, flour 84 %, soda 1 % (m/m)", "15 : 84 : 1 %"],
 ["Brass with 70 % copper in 120 g", "Copper = 0.70 × 120; zinc = the rest", "84 g Cu, 36 g Zn"],
 ["20 g sugar in 80 g water (A); 20 g in 100 g (B); 30 g in 80 g (C)", "A: 20/100 = 20 %; B: 20/120 = 16.7 %; C: 30/110 = 27.3 %", "C is most concentrated (most solute per 100 g solution)"]]),
("note", "REMEMBER", "A saline drip in hospitals is **0.9 % m/v** sodium chloride — 0.9 g of salt in every 100 mL — which is safe for blood and replaces lost body fluids."),

("h2", "2.3 Solubility"),
("ul", ["**Solubility** = the maximum amount of solute that dissolves in a fixed quantity of solvent (100 g or 100 mL) at a given temperature. A solution that cannot dissolve any more solute at that temperature is a **saturated solution**.",
 "The temperature is always mentioned because solubility depends on it. For **solids in liquids**, solubility generally **increases** with temperature; for **gases in liquids**, it generally **decreases** with temperature (which is why a warm soda goes flat).",
 "A graph of solubility (g per 100 g water, y-axis) against temperature (°C, x-axis) is a **solubility curve**. A steeper curve means solubility changes more with temperature."]),
("h3", "Reading solubility data (Table 5.4)"),
("table", ["Salt (g per 100 g water)", "10 °C", "20 °C", "30 °C", "40 °C", "60 °C", "80 °C", "Trend"], [
 ["Potassium nitrate", "21", "32", "45", "62", "106", "167", "Rises very steeply"],
 ["Sodium chloride", "36", "36", "36.3", "36.5", "37", "37", "Almost unchanged"],
 ["Potassium chloride", "35", "35", "37.4", "40", "46", "54", "Rises moderately"],
 ["Ammonium chloride", "24", "37", "41", "41", "55", "66", "Rises steadily"]]),
("ul", ["Saturated KNO<sub>3</sub> solution in 50 g water at 40 °C needs 62 ÷ 2 = **31 g**.",
 "Cooling a saturated KCl solution from 80 °C to 25 °C: solubility falls from 54 to about 35 g, so about 19 g per 100 g water separates out as **crystals**.",
 "Because NaCl's solubility hardly changes with temperature, cooling cannot crystallize it effectively — salt is obtained by **evaporation** instead."]),

("h2", "2.4 Separating Homogeneous Mixtures"),
("h3", "A. Crystallization"),
("ul", ["A **crystal** is a solid whose particles are arranged in a regular geometric pattern (rock salt, sugar crystals/mishri, snowflakes, frost, quartz, copper sulfate).",
 "**Crystallization** = forming crystals from a hot saturated solution on cooling. **Principle:** solubility of a substance differs at different temperatures. Example: a saturated solution of compound B made at 60 °C (287 g per 100 g water) when cooled to 40 °C can hold only 241 g, so the extra ~46 g separates as pure solid.",
 "Used to separate two solids soluble in the same solvent when one is present in small amount, and to **purify** solids (separating a desired pure substance from unwanted impurities)."]),
("steps", [("Step 1 — Saturate", "Dissolve about 1 g copper sulfate in 25 mL water in a beaker with a drop of dilute sulfuric acid (added by the teacher; it helps give pure crystals); warm in a water bath, stirring, adding more copper sulfate until no more dissolves."),
 ("Step 2 — Filter hot", "Filter the hot saturated solution to remove insoluble impurities; collect the filtrate in a clean beaker and cover with a watch glass."),
 ("Step 3 — Cool slowly", "Leave undisturbed. Slow cooling gives particles time to join, forming **larger, shiny, well-shaped blue crystals**; rapid cooling (ice-cold water) gives small, poorly formed crystals."),
 ("Step 4 — Collect", "Filter the crystals, rinse with cold water and dry on a watch glass. Copper sulfate is toxic — work under adult supervision.")]),
("ul", ["**Salt from seawater:** seawater is held in shallow ponds, the sun evaporates water, a saturated solution forms and salt crystals deposit. Ancient Indian methods: *panga* salt (boiling concentrated brine) and *karkatch* salt (solar evaporation of sea water) — giving crystals of different sizes.",
 "Natural crystals: Mawsmai Cave (Sohra/Cherrapunji) formations and quartz."]),
("table", ["", "Evaporation", "Crystallization", "Distillation"], [
 ["What is obtained", "Solute only (solvent lost)", "Pure crystals of the solute", "Solvent/liquid (also a liquid from a liquid mix)"],
 ["Process", "Solvent vaporises, solid left behind", "Hot saturated solution is cooled; crystals form", "Heat, vaporise lower-boiling liquid, condense"],
 ["Prefer when", "Only the solid is needed; it is not affected by heat (e.g. salt)", "Pure, well-formed solid needed; solubility changes with temperature; solid may decompose on heating", "The solvent or the lower-boiling liquid is also to be recovered"]]),
("h3", "B. Distillation"),
("ul", ["A homogeneous mixture of **two miscible liquids** is heated until the liquid with the **lower boiling point** vaporises; its vapour is cooled in a **condenser** and collected as the pure **distillate**. The other liquid (or dissolved solid) stays in the distillation flask.",
 "Works when boiling points differ by **at least about 25 °C**. Also used to recover a liquid from a solution containing dissolved solids (e.g. pure water from salt water).",
 "**Set-up:** distillation flask with thermometer on a tripod/wire gauze and burner → water condenser (water enters at the bottom inlet, leaves from the top outlet) → conical flask to collect distillate.",
 "Acetone (b.p. 56 °C) and water (100 °C) separate easily: acetone distils first."]),
("table", ["Compound", "Water", "Acetone", "Alcohol", "Chloroform", "Benzene"], [["Boiling point", "100 °C", "56 °C", "78 °C", "61 °C", "80 °C"]]),
("ul", ["With Table 5.5: water–acetone (44 °C gap) can be separated by simple distillation; acetone–alcohol (22 °C), alcohol–chloroform (17 °C) and alcohol–benzene (2 °C) have gaps smaller than 25 °C and need **fractional distillation**; sand–salt are two solids and need dissolving/filtering/evaporation.",
 "**Fractional distillation** separates liquids with small boiling-point differences (less than 25 °C). Crude petroleum is separated in a refinery into petroleum gas (filled in cylinders and liquefied as **LPG**), petrol, aviation fuel (kerosene), diesel, lubricating oil and bitumen.",
 "**Kannauj (Uttar Pradesh)**, the perfume capital of India, captures the smell of first rain as *mitti ka ittar* using the traditional **Deg-Bhapka** distillation method."]),
("h3", "C. Paper chromatography"),
("ul", ["The word comes from Greek *chroma* (colour) and *graphein* (to write) — first used to separate dyes and inks.",
 "**Principle:** components differ in how strongly they interact with the paper and the solvent, so they travel at **different rates** and separate into spots."]),
("steps", [("Step 1 — Spot", "On a 3 cm wide chromatography (or filter) paper strip draw a pencil line 2 cm from the bottom; put a spot of black sketch-pen ink at its centre."),
 ("Step 2 — Develop", "Dip the lower end in a thin layer of water in a gas jar/beaker, hanging vertically. The water level must be **below the spot**."),
 ("Step 3 — Observe", "As water rises it carries the colours along; the ink splits into different coloured spots. Faster-moving colours travel higher.")]),
("ul", ["Can be applied to green food colour (with 2 % m/v salt solution as solvent), spinach pigments, flower pigments, fennel-seed coloured freshener. Water does not suit every case — alcohol or a solvent mixture may be needed.",
 "A pencil (not ink) is used for the baseline because graphite does not dissolve or move with the solvent."]),

("h2", "2.5 Separating Heterogeneous Mixtures"),
("h3", "A. Two immiscible liquids — separating funnel"),
("ul", ["Immiscible liquids (oil and water) form two layers and are separated by **density difference**.",
 "Pour the mixture into a separating funnel and let it stand. Mustard oil (less dense) forms the **upper** layer; water the **lower**. Open the stopcock slowly to drain the water; close it when nearly all water is out; collect the in-between mixed portion separately and discard it; then collect the oil.",
 "Cooking oil (910 g per litre) is lighter than water (1000 g per litre), so it floats on top. If two immiscible liquids had the **same density**, they would not form distinct layers and the funnel would fail.",
 "Mixtures with gases: gases mix freely, so most gas mixtures are homogeneous (air, hydrogen + oxygen rocket fuel). **Smoke** (solid in air), **fog** (liquid droplets in air) and dust in air are heterogeneous."]),
("h3", "B. Sublimation (solid–solid mixtures)"),
("ul", ["**Sublimation:** a solid changes directly to vapour on heating, below its melting point, without passing through the liquid state. **Deposition:** vapour cools and turns directly into solid.",
 "**Activity:** heat a camphor–sand mixture gently in a china dish with an inverted funnel (nozzle plugged with cotton) on top. Camphor sublimes and deposits as white solid on the cool funnel wall; sand stays behind. Naphthalene and ammonium chloride behave similarly; dry ice (solid CO<sub>2</sub>) also sublimes.",
 "Sublimation differs from evaporation: evaporation is liquid → vapour; sublimation is solid → vapour directly.",
 "Sequence for sand + salt + naphthalene: (1) **sublimation** removes naphthalene; (2) add water and **filter** to leave sand; (3) **evaporate/crystallize** the filtrate to get salt."]),
("h3", "Alloys"),
("ul", ["Metals do not dissolve in each other at room temperature, but molten metals can mix into a solution which solidifies on cooling. An **alloy** is a homogeneous mixture of two or more metals, or of a metal and a non-metal. Its components **cannot be separated by physical methods**.",
 "Made to get materials that are stronger, more rigid or more corrosion-resistant."]),
("table", ["Alloy", "Composition"], [["Brass", "About 80 % copper, 20 % zinc"], ["Bronze", "About 80 % copper, 20 % tin"], ["Stainless steel", "Iron with carbon (0.03–0.8 %), chromium (16–18 %), nickel (10–14 %), molybdenum (2–3 %)"]]),

("h2", "2.6 Suspensions"),
("ul", ["A **suspension** is a heterogeneous mixture in which insoluble solid particles remain spread (suspended) through the medium. Particles are **visible to the naked eye** and larger than 1000 nm; they settle on standing. Examples: sand, sawdust or tea leaves in water; muddy water.",
 "Filtering through cloth or filter paper removes only larger particles — the water often stays cloudy, so further methods are needed."]),
("h3", "Centrifugation"),
("ul", ["Spinning a mixture in a tube at high speed: the tubes become horizontal and **centrifugal force** (the outward force on a body moving in a circle) pushes heavier particles to the bottom; the lighter liquid remains on top. (Feel it in the partner-spinning game *phugadi*, a folk dance.)",
 "Used in labs to separate blood into **red blood cells, white cells, platelets and plasma**, and in chemical industries.",
 "**Paperfuge:** a hand-powered device based on a toy (cardboard disc, string, handles) that separates blood without electricity; helps detect malaria and anaemia in remote areas.",
 "Blood donation: donated blood is tested, its group identified and it is separated into plasma, platelets, white and red cells for storage in blood banks; the body replaces donated blood in a few weeks."]),
("h3", "Coagulation"),
("ul", ["Adding **alum (fitkari)** to muddy water makes fine suspended particles clump together; the alum is the **coagulant**. The larger clumps settle by gravity (**sedimentation**) and the clear water is then separated by **decantation** or filtration.",
 "Everyday example: making **paneer** — lemon juice or vinegar (acid) coagulates milk proteins.",
 "Sewage treatment uses sedimentation, coagulation and filtration; the clean water can be reused for flushing or watering plants."]),

("h2", "2.7 Colloids and the Tyndall Effect"),
("ul", ["Blood, milk, tomato sauce and ice cream are **colloids** — neither true solutions nor suspensions. Particle size is **1–1000 nm**; the particles are too small to see with the naked eye, do **not settle**, and are uniformly dispersed (so a colloid looks homogeneous).",
 "Components: the **dispersed phase** (the solute-like particles) and the **dispersion medium** (the substance in which they are spread). In blood sampled for centrifugation the cells/platelets are the dispersed phase and plasma is the medium. If blood behaved like a true suspension, the cells would settle and could not circulate to carry nutrients, gases and hormones.",
 "**Emulsions:** colloids in which both phases are liquids. *Oil-in-water* (milk, vanishing cream); *water-in-oil* (butter, cold cream, body lotion). **Emulsifying agents** (e.g. milk proteins) stabilise them. Shake oil with soap solution to make one.",
 "Clouds (tiny water droplets/ice crystals in air) are colloids; smoke and dust make city air hazy by scattering light."]),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Tyndall effect:** the scattering of light by particles in a medium, making the path of a light beam visible. Named after John Tyndall. Seen in a sunbeam entering a dark room (dust/smoke), through forest leaves, and in stadium floodlights."),
("table", ["Property", "Solution", "Suspension", "Colloid"], [
 ["Nature", "Homogeneous", "Heterogeneous", "Appears homogeneous, actually heterogeneous"],
 ["Particle size", "Less than 1 nm", "More than 1000 nm", "1–1000 nm"],
 ["Visibility", "Particles not visible; transparent", "Particles visible; opaque/cloudy", "Particles not visible; translucent/milky"],
 ["Filtration", "Passes through filter paper", "Residue stays on filter paper", "Passes through filter paper"],
 ["Settling on standing", "No", "Yes", "No"],
 ["Tyndall effect", "No", "Yes", "Yes"],
 ["Examples", "Salt solution, brass, vinegar, air", "Sand/mud in water, chalk in water", "Milk, butter, smoke, blood, tomato sauce"]]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "Assertion–reason favourite: *Solutions do not show the Tyndall effect* is true, but the reason is **not** that solute particles are larger than 100 nm — they are **smaller than 1 nm** and too small to scatter light. Mixtures showing the effect: air with dust and starch in water; not copper sulfate or acetone in water."),

("h2", "2.8 Separation in Real Life"),
("ul", ["Kidneys separate waste from blood; sewage treatment, ocean/river plastic clean-up and **waste segregation** (dry waste recycled, wet waste composted) all rely on separation methods.",
 "Researchers recover valuable materials such as **lithium** from old mobile-phone and laptop batteries."]),
("table", ["Mixture", "Method", "Reason"], [
 ["Mud from muddy water", "Sedimentation, decantation/filtration (coagulation if fine)", "Insoluble particles larger than water"],
 ["Plasma from blood", "Centrifugation", "Different densities"],
 ["Naphthalene and sand", "Sublimation", "Naphthalene sublimes, sand does not"],
 ["Chalk powder and salt", "Add water, filter, evaporate filtrate", "Salt dissolves, chalk does not"],
 ["Salt and water", "Evaporation (or distillation to also get water)", "Salt is non-volatile"],
 ["Oil and water", "Separating funnel", "Immiscible; different densities"],
 ["Pigments of a flower", "Paper chromatography", "Different rates of movement on paper"],
 ["Acetone (60 °C) and another liquid (90 °C) — miscible", "Simple distillation", "Boiling points differ by 30 °C (>25 °C)"]]),

("qa", [
 ("Differentiate between a solution, suspension and colloid.", "Particle size: <1 nm, >1000 nm, 1–1000 nm. Solutions are clear, do not settle, no Tyndall effect; suspensions settle, are filtered and scatter light; colloids do not settle, pass filter paper and scatter light."),
 ("Define Tyndall effect and give two examples.", "Scattering of light by particles in a medium so that the beam’s path is visible; sunbeam in a dusty room, headlight beam in fog or stadium floodlights."),
 ("Write the three formulae for concentration in percentage.", "% m/m = (solute mass ÷ solution mass) × 100; % m/v = (solute mass ÷ solution volume) × 100; % v/v = (solute volume ÷ solution volume) × 100."),
 ("What is solubility? How does temperature affect it?", "Maximum solute dissolving in 100 g solvent at a given temperature; solids’ solubility generally increases, gases’ decreases with temperature."),
 ("Why is slow cooling preferred in crystallization?", "It lets particles arrange gradually, giving large, well-shaped, pure crystals."),
 ("When is distillation used? What is the condition?", "To separate miscible liquids (or a liquid from a dissolved solid); boiling points must differ by at least ~25 °C; otherwise use fractional distillation."),
 ("Explain paper chromatography.", "Solvent rises through paper, carrying components at different rates because of different interactions with paper and solvent, forming separate spots; the baseline spot must stay above the solvent."),
 ("Why does oil float on water and how are they separated?", "Oil is less dense; use a separating funnel and drain the lower water layer first."),
 ("What is sublimation? Name two substances that sublime.", "Solid to vapour directly on heating; camphor, naphthalene (also ammonium chloride, dry ice)."),
 ("How do centrifugation and coagulation differ?", "Centrifugation uses rapid spinning (centrifugal force) to settle heavier particles; coagulation uses a coagulant such as alum to clump fine particles which then settle."),
 ("What is an alloy? Give composition of brass.", "Homogeneous mixture of metals (or a metal and non-metal); brass ≈ 80 % copper, 20 % zinc."),
 ("What is an emulsion? Give one example of each type.", "Colloid of two liquids; milk (oil-in-water), butter (water-in-oil).")]),
],
takeaways=["Mixtures are homogeneous (solutions) or heterogeneous; by particle size they are solutions (<1 nm), colloids (1–1000 nm) or suspensions (>1000 nm).",
 "Concentration is expressed as % m/m, % m/v or % v/v; mass of solution = solute + solvent.",
 "Solubility of solids rises with temperature, of gases falls; crystallization exploits this.",
 "Distillation (≥25 °C difference), fractional distillation, chromatography, separating funnel, sublimation, centrifugation and coagulation each suit a specific kind of mixture.",
 "Colloids and suspensions scatter light (Tyndall effect); true solutions do not.",
 "Alloys are homogeneous mixtures that cannot be separated physically."])
